// The terrainlod package: ground that refines itself with distance, over
// any height function or heightmap tiles, drawn by the render package. A
// program reaches it with 'import terrainlod', and gets:
//
// - Terrain (terrain.cone), a CDLOD quadtree (Strugar 2010): the ground
//   near the camera in a fine grid, farther out in grids half as fine at
//   each doubling of distance, vertices that slide (geomorph) from one
//   level to the next with distance so nothing pops, and borders between
//   levels that meet exactly so nothing cracks. A far horizon of tens of
//   kilometres costs a few hundred draws, the same however far, one more
//   level for each doubling. 'TerrainConfig', 'TerrainLook' (layers of
//   colour by height and steepness, haze) and 'TerrainStats' go with it.
// - The numbers (lodmath.cone): 'LodLevels', the ranges and morph bands;
//   the CPU's twin of the shader's geomorph.
// - Height sources (source.cone): a function is a closure
//   'fn(x f32, z f32) f32'; 'HeightTiles' holds heightmap tiles and answers
//   one.
// - Queries (queries.cone) on the same function for physics: the finest
//   drawn surface's height and normal at a point, a ray cast.
//
// A frame, with render's Renderer:
//
//   mut terrain = Terrain.create(&mut device, &mut renderer, cfg);         // once
//   terrain.update(&mut device, &mut renderer, &camera, width, height, &height);   // each frame
//   terrain.draw(&mut list);                                               // into the DrawList
//   renderer.render(&mut device, &mut encoder, &target, &depth, &camera, &light, &list);
//
// where 'height' is the closure the ground comes from.
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports.

mod terrainlod;

import libc;
import geomath use *;
import collections use *;
import mesh use Mesh;
import gpu use *;
import render use *;
import time use Instant;
